# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
hume-miracles.md:1: argue/readings: S1 is read 2 ways: hume-miracles.logic.r2 and hume-miracles.logic.r2.3 have "∀x (miracle(x) → violates(x))" and "∀x (violates(x) → uniform(x))"; hume-miracles.logic.r2.2 has "∀x (miracle(x) ↔ violates(x))" and "∀x (violates(x) → uniform(x))"; they differ when nothing is true, which the first allows
hume-miracles.md:1: argue/answered: S1: answered no, which keeps hume-miracles.logic.r2.2, to: Suppose nothing is true. Is S1, "A miracle is a violation of the laws of nature; and as a firm and unalterable experience has established these laws, the proof against a miracle, from the very nature of the fact, is as entire as any argument from experience can possibly be imagined.", true then?
hume-miracles.md:1: argue/readings: S7 is read 3 ways: hume-miracles.logic.r2 has "∀x (uniform(x) → proof(x))" and "∀x (miracle(x) ∧ proof(x) → (credible(x) → superior(x)))"; hume-miracles.logic.r2.2 has "∀x (uniform(x) → proof(x))" and "∀x (proof(x) ∧ ¬superior(x) → ¬credible(x))"; they differ when nothing is true, which the first allows; hume-miracles.logic.r2.3 has "∀x (uniform(x) → proof(x))" and "∀x (miracle(x) ∧ credible(x) → superior(x))"
hume-miracles.md:1: argue/answered: S7: answered no, which keeps hume-miracles.logic.r2.2, to: Suppose nothing is true. Is S7, "And as a uniform experience amounts to a proof, there is here a direct and full proof, from the nature of the fact, against the existence of any miracle; nor can such a proof be destroyed, or the miracle rendered credible, but by an opposite proof, which is superior.", true then?
hume-miracles.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 2 ways: hume-miracles.logic.r2 and hume-miracles.logic.r2.3 have "∀x (miracle(x) → proof(x) ∧ (credible(x) → superior(x)))"; hume-miracles.logic.r2.2 has "∀x (miracle(x) → proof(x))"; they differ when nothing is true, which the second allows
hume-miracles.md:1: argue/answered: the conclusion: answered no, which keeps hume-miracles.logic.r2 and hume-miracles.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose nothing is true. Is the conclusion, "And as a uniform experience amounts to a proof, there is here a direct and full proof, from the nature of the fact, against the existence of any miracle; nor can such a proof be destroyed, or the miracle rendered credible, but by an opposite proof, which is superior.", true then?
